  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 315 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nFor the start of the week, continued ridging is expected from Texas \nnortheast into the Great Lakes.  Across the NE US, an upper level \nand associated surface low will continue to lift northeast into SE \nCanada.  Out across the west, an strong shortwave trough axis in the \nsouthern stream will move thorugh the southwest US and continue to \ndeepen.  Locally, we'll continue to be under the effects of the \nridging aloft.  So a continued dry weather pattern is expected. With \nsurface high pressure located to our east, we'll get into a return \nflow pattern with southerly winds bringing milder temperatures to \nthe region.  Highs on Monday will warm into the mid-upper 70s, \nthough a few spots west of I-65 could top out around 80.  Overnight \nlows Monday night will be around 50.  Highs on Tuesday should \nmoderate a little more with readings in the upper 70s to the lower \n80s.\n\nWednesday and Thursday...\n\nBy mid-week, focus will turn to the southwest US where a closed \nupper level low in the southern stream will start to roll eastward. \nAs this occurs, a northern stream shortwave trough will push out of \nthe Rockies and into the central Plains.  The flow is a bit faster \nin the northern stream and it appears that the northern stream wave \nwill quickly work into the Great Lakes by Thursday.  A slow moving \ncold frontal boundary will work eastward across the Plains but will \nremain west of the region through at least Wednesday.  Current \nblended PoPs are likely too wet for Wednesday and I think we'll see \nthose PoPs get scaled back in successive runs.  It does appear that \nthe cold front may get into region by Thursday...perhaps as late as \nFriday/Saturday and stall out across the region as the flow aloft \nbecome more zonal out ahead of the slow moving wave coming out of \nthe Plains.  For now, we'll continue to highlight chance PoPs in the \nThursday period.  Highs on Wednesday will range from the upper 70s \nto the lower 80s over southern Indiana and the northern half of \nKentucky.  Southern Kentucky will likely see temps warm into the \nmiddle 80s.  Similar readings are expected for Thursday, though \nexpected clouds and possible precip, may impact temps here.\n\nExtended Forecast Discussion...\n\nMoving into late next week, the slow moving upper level low looks to \nopen up and eventually head eastward.  Strong southwest flow may \nbring another round of warm air just ahead of the feature.  Current \nensemble guidance suggests that unsettled weather will likely be \nseen across the region next weekend.  As that system heads east, a \nstrong ridge is expected to develop across the western US with \ndownstream troughing across the eastern US.  Model solutions have \nbeen trending stronger with this wave and a blast of cold air may \ninfiltrate the Ohio Valley on/after 10/6 or so and linger for \nseveral days.  The pattern looks likely to relax a bit.  However, \nwe'll see a west Pacific typhoon (Surigae) recurve and merge into \nthe Pacific Jet as we cross into October.   This is likely to \nperturb the jet a bit and lead to a stronger west coast Ridge and \ndeeper eastern US trough with another surge of cool air arriving \naround the middle of October.  Longer range teleconnection patterns \ngoing into Mid-October feature a -AO/-NAO/+PNA pattern with the EPO \nflipping from positive to negative around mid-month.  This would \nsuggest that colder than normal conditions may develop/continue \nacross the Ohio Valley from Mid-October onward.",
